import { FilesystemReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> FilesystemReaderCopy = FilesystemReaderCopy {
  summary: "Add one filename to a directory Path, rejecting names that include directory traversal or separators.",
  taskTitle: "Add a report filename to a directory",
  purpose: "Add report.txt to the destination reports. The caller should supply a filename only, so reject an input such as ../report.txt that moves between directories. Dots within a name, as in report.v2.txt, remain valid.",
  setup: "Import std/path as paths. fn addFilename base: paths.Path -> name: String -> String declares a function taking a Path called base and a String called name, returning display text. The first -> separates the two parameters; the final -> introduces the return type. The argument order of paths.child name base is filename, then directory. Do not assume it matches the helper’s parameter order. match selects Left or Right from the result, and Right file receives the completed Path.",
  after: [
    "The first two inputs produce reports/report.txt and reports/report.v2.txt. ../report.txt, empty text, ., and .. are rejected. The _ in Left _ ignores the failure reason for this display; the input name is printed after rejected: instead.",
    "paths.parse \"reports\" also returns Left reason for failure or Right base for success. Only success proceeds to addFilename. paths.render file turns the completed Path into text, while show reason turns a validation problem into text. ${name} inside backticks inserts name into the string.",
    "pub effect fn main is the execution entry point. Within do, each _ <- println (...) completes one print before continuing; the final println follows in the same order. addFilename base \"report.txt\" supplies two arguments separated by spaces. Constructing these paths does not require a reports directory to exist."
  ],
  details: [
    "child rejects empty text, ., .., and names containing /, a backslash, or NUL (the zero character) with InvalidPathSegment. report.v2.txt is accepted because the entire name is neither . nor .. and has no separator. If you intend to accept directory components, distinguish that task from this one-filename operation.",
    "The return type Either<PathError, Path> describes a failure reason carried by Left or a successful Path carried by Right. The base must already be a checked Path. To obtain one from a String, handle the result of parse as the example does.",
    "Restricting an input to one name does not guarantee confinement to a safe directory. The chosen base, permissions, and symbolic links determine the actual destination. child neither checks existence nor creates a directory."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "The TS example tries the same six inputs. Its if checks reject empty text, ., and .., while a regular expression rejects separators and NUL before constructing the string. Node’s path.join alone also accepts ../report.txt, so it would not implement the same input contract.",
  comparisonAfter: "Both examples implement the same filename conditions and display results. In Seseragi, child owns the check and returns a Path on success. A short TS validator can already do this job; the main differences are the result type and the shared library operation."
}
